00:03:09.633 --> 00:03:10.550
KOBI MAGNEZI: Awesome.

00:03:10.550 --> 00:03:12.410
So let's look at
several recommendation

00:03:12.410 --> 00:03:15.300
we have for day zero.

00:03:15.300 --> 00:03:18.310
And when thinking about high
availability of GKE cluster,

00:03:18.310 --> 00:03:20.140
it is important
to carefully plan

00:03:20.140 --> 00:03:23.200
the topology and configuration
of your GKE cluster

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:25.780
before you even
deploy your workloads.

00:03:25.780 --> 00:03:29.110
Let's look quickly on
the GKE architecture

00:03:29.110 --> 00:03:30.760
and main components.

00:03:30.760 --> 00:03:33.070
When you provision a
new Kubernetes cluster,

00:03:33.070 --> 00:03:35.620
GKE provisions the
machine, the underlying GCE

00:03:35.620 --> 00:03:38.380
resources that are
required for the control

00:03:38.380 --> 00:03:40.120
plane and the nodes.

00:03:40.120 --> 00:03:42.820
We also manage the rollout
of the Kubernetes version

00:03:42.820 --> 00:03:46.270
and patches, and their
availability in the GKE fleet.

00:03:46.270 --> 00:03:49.660
GKE manages the control
plane, including

00:03:49.660 --> 00:03:52.840
all the components running on
it, things like API server,

00:03:52.840 --> 00:03:54.440
[INAUDIBLE], and others.

00:03:54.440 --> 00:03:57.040
We also manage the software
that runs on the node.

00:03:57.040 --> 00:04:01.760
That includes operating system
image, kubelet, and others.

00:04:01.760 --> 00:04:03.290
When you create a
cluster, you can

00:04:03.290 --> 00:04:05.850
decide on the right
topology for your business.

00:04:05.850 --> 00:04:09.290
You can choose to create a zonal
cluster, which includes one

00:04:09.290 --> 00:04:10.982
replica of your control plane.

00:04:10.982 --> 00:04:12.440
And then you can
decide whether you

00:04:12.440 --> 00:04:15.980
want to have all your
nodes spanned across three

00:04:15.980 --> 00:04:17.690
different zones or
whether you want

00:04:17.690 --> 00:04:20.810
to keep them all in one
zone with the control plane.

00:04:20.810 --> 00:04:24.590
That really depends on your
needs from the environment.

00:04:24.590 --> 00:04:27.740
The alternative is to create a
regional cluster, for which GKE

00:04:27.740 --> 00:04:31.130
provisions three machines
for the control plane.

00:04:31.130 --> 00:04:33.680
And the node pools are
also spanned across three

00:04:33.680 --> 00:04:35.150
availability zones.

00:04:35.150 --> 00:04:37.040
The control plane
redundancy provide

00:04:37.040 --> 00:04:39.240
a high-availability
control plane,

00:04:39.240 --> 00:04:44.200
which, in turn, offer higher
SLA for the Kubernetes API.

00:04:44.200 --> 00:04:45.910
Even though your
business application,

00:04:45.910 --> 00:04:48.760
those that run on the nodes,
will continue to run and serve

00:04:48.760 --> 00:04:50.590
traffic when the
control plane is

00:04:50.590 --> 00:04:54.220
down temporarily, for
example, for maintenance,

00:04:54.220 --> 00:04:56.170
it is recommended to
use regional cluster

00:04:56.170 --> 00:04:58.100
for highly available workloads.

00:04:58.100 --> 00:05:01.600
Kubernetes control plane is
responsible to observe, scale,

00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:03.290
and repair your cluster.

00:05:03.290 --> 00:05:05.410
And when higher
availability is required,

00:05:05.410 --> 00:05:08.050
regional clusters
should be preferred.

00:05:08.050 --> 00:05:10.170
Whichever topology
you end up using,

00:05:10.170 --> 00:05:12.400
GKE offer a fully
managed experience

00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:14.050
for the control plane.

00:05:14.050 --> 00:05:15.970
That means that we
take care of backups,

00:05:15.970 --> 00:05:18.460
repairing the control
plane if something happen.

00:05:18.460 --> 00:05:21.370
We make sure it's available
through monitoring,

00:05:21.370 --> 00:05:23.860
health checks, and auto-repair.

00:05:23.860 --> 00:05:26.710
We also keep you up to date, and
we upgrade your control planes

00:05:26.710 --> 00:05:29.650
to the latest Kubernetes
version and security patch

00:05:29.650 --> 00:05:31.870
available in a reliable manner.

00:05:31.870 --> 00:05:35.080
And most importantly,
you get Google SRE

00:05:35.080 --> 00:05:38.930
to monitor and manage your
infrastructure for you.

00:05:38.930 --> 00:05:40.890
Nodes are a shared
responsibility.

00:05:40.890 --> 00:05:43.205
That means that while the
control plane is hosted

00:05:43.205 --> 00:05:47.060
in a Google project, the
nodes are run in your project,

00:05:47.060 --> 00:05:48.530
in the customer project.

00:05:48.530 --> 00:05:52.100
That being said, GKE offers
a variety of capabilities

00:05:52.100 --> 00:05:54.920
that automate the lifecycle
management of the nodes

00:05:54.920 --> 00:05:58.360
as well, and we'll
cover those next.

00:05:58.360 --> 00:06:02.130
So now that you decided
on the cluster topology,

00:06:02.130 --> 00:06:04.970
it is important to consider
the scaling strategy.

00:06:04.970 --> 00:06:07.230
While scalability is
important for continuity,

00:06:07.230 --> 00:06:11.590
scaling is necessary to
ensure that as you grow,

00:06:11.590 --> 00:06:12.850
we match your needs.

00:06:12.850 --> 00:06:15.750
So we can grow the number
of pods, the number of nodes

00:06:15.750 --> 00:06:17.340
based on demand.

00:06:17.340 --> 00:06:19.590
GKE includes several
auto-scaling capabilities

00:06:19.590 --> 00:06:23.400
that allow GKE to automatically
resize your cluster based

00:06:23.400 --> 00:06:28.780
on your configuration and
some monitored signals.

00:06:28.780 --> 00:06:31.110
Donovan, I would love
to hear how Dexcom

00:06:31.110 --> 00:06:33.675
is using auto-scaling as well.

00:06:33.675 --> 00:06:34.800
DONOVAN CARTER: Absolutely.

00:06:34.800 --> 00:06:37.590
Dexcom's grown tremendously,
and being a part of it

00:06:37.590 --> 00:06:39.540
has been an awesome experience.

00:06:39.540 --> 00:06:42.780
But part of that comes
with design and planning

00:06:42.780 --> 00:06:45.390
and specifically having
to scale up resources.

00:06:45.390 --> 00:06:48.700
And being a part of a cloud
provider such as Google really,

00:06:48.700 --> 00:06:49.980
really helps with that.

00:06:49.980 --> 00:06:52.230
Things that we don't have
to think about things thanks

00:06:52.230 --> 00:06:54.090
to the GKE cluster
auto-scalar are,

00:06:54.090 --> 00:06:56.490
do we have enough nodes
for our workloads?

00:06:56.490 --> 00:06:58.460
Thanks for the GKE
cluster auto-scalar,

00:06:58.460 --> 00:06:59.910
that problem is solved.

00:06:59.910 --> 00:07:02.220
It brings in new nodes
whenever we need them.

00:07:02.220 --> 00:07:04.590
Additionally, we've been able
to leverage horizontal pod

00:07:04.590 --> 00:07:07.680
auto-scaling in concert
with the cluster auto-scalar

00:07:07.680 --> 00:07:10.590
to provide a better quality
of service to our customers

00:07:10.590 --> 00:07:13.170
during peak utilization hours.

00:07:13.170 --> 00:07:16.750
Being a worldwide company, that
can be at any time of the day

00:07:16.750 --> 00:07:18.970
depending on what
region we're looking at.

00:07:18.970 --> 00:07:20.880
So having to have a
person monitor that

00:07:20.880 --> 00:07:23.620
and manually intervene
just doesn't make sense.

00:07:23.620 --> 00:07:26.220
We've also seen the opposite.

00:07:26.220 --> 00:07:28.920
When we're at our valley
utilization period,

00:07:28.920 --> 00:07:33.000
we're able to save money as
well as resources for Google

00:07:33.000 --> 00:07:35.220
by scaling down to an
acceptable threshold

00:07:35.220 --> 00:07:37.410
to provide the same
quality of service

00:07:37.410 --> 00:07:40.968
and not use those resources,
which has been wonderful.

00:07:40.968 --> 00:07:42.510
KOBI MAGNEZI: And
auto-scaling really

00:07:42.510 --> 00:07:46.830
help you with scaling up and
down to meet your demand,

00:07:46.830 --> 00:07:49.180
but sometimes we do need to
do some capacity planning,

00:07:49.180 --> 00:07:51.990
especially when you
expect some peak demand

00:07:51.990 --> 00:07:56.040
seasons like Black Friday,
Cyber Monday, or New Year.

00:07:56.040 --> 00:07:58.470
In this period of
time, we may want

00:07:58.470 --> 00:08:00.660
to reserve GCE
capacity so that when

00:08:00.660 --> 00:08:03.390
we need that capacity--
for example, for scaling,

00:08:03.390 --> 00:08:05.610
repairing, or
upgrading-- we'll have

00:08:05.610 --> 00:08:08.250
a reserved pool of resources
available for the cluster

00:08:08.250 --> 00:08:09.240
to consume.

00:08:09.240 --> 00:08:12.960
Basically, GKE reservation
is an integration between GKE

00:08:12.960 --> 00:08:15.880
and GCE zonal reservation.

00:08:15.880 --> 00:08:19.290
So you can create a specific
or non-specific reservation,

00:08:19.290 --> 00:08:21.720
and GKE will create
new resources based

00:08:21.720 --> 00:08:23.940
on the pool reserved for you.

00:08:23.940 --> 00:08:25.890
There's an example
here on the right side

00:08:25.890 --> 00:08:28.950
where we create reservation
of three machines,

00:08:28.950 --> 00:08:30.810
we verify the
reservation, and then

00:08:30.810 --> 00:08:33.780
we create a cluster to consume
any matching reservation

00:08:33.780 --> 00:08:34.990
that we created.

00:08:34.990 --> 00:08:37.200
Now that we covered the
provisioning and scaling

00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:40.049
of nodes, let's talk about
the application, your business

00:08:40.049 --> 00:08:42.929
application, those that
run on the nodes, different

00:08:42.929 --> 00:08:45.820
application and defined
behavior and needs.

00:08:45.820 --> 00:08:49.380
Some applications are stateless,
and some are stateful.

00:08:49.380 --> 00:08:52.920
More than 50% of GKE customers
run stateful workloads

00:08:52.920 --> 00:08:55.470
on Kubernetes successfully.

00:08:55.470 --> 00:08:59.830
It's not an easy task, but
luckily, Kubernetes and GKE

00:08:59.830 --> 00:09:03.150
offer configuration
and tools to support

00:09:03.150 --> 00:09:04.950
different type of application.

00:09:04.950 --> 00:09:08.633
Donovan, do you guys run any
stateful application on GKE?

00:09:08.633 --> 00:09:09.550
DONOVAN CARTER: We do.

00:09:09.550 --> 00:09:12.120
And when you say it's not
easy, you're absolutely right.

00:09:12.120 --> 00:09:16.500
There are certainly things
that make it difficult or less

00:09:16.500 --> 00:09:19.290
difficult, and I think we're
going to get into those here

00:09:19.290 --> 00:09:21.040
in just a second.

00:09:21.040 --> 00:09:22.180
KOBI MAGNEZI: Exactly.

00:09:22.180 --> 00:09:24.590
And when you run different
types of application,

00:09:24.590 --> 00:09:26.890
you also need to
think about disruption

00:09:26.890 --> 00:09:29.030
and how you can
tolerate disruption.

00:09:29.030 --> 00:09:32.350
Pod Disruption
Budget, or PDB, allows

00:09:32.350 --> 00:09:34.450
you to state your
disruption tolerance.

00:09:34.450 --> 00:09:37.060
In other words, if your
application is, let's say,

00:09:37.060 --> 00:09:39.320
replicated, you can
specify how many

00:09:39.320 --> 00:09:42.010
replicas you are
willing to sacrifice

00:09:42.010 --> 00:09:45.280
when voluntary events,
like upgrade, happens.

00:09:45.280 --> 00:09:48.620
In this example on
the right side here,

00:09:48.620 --> 00:09:51.670
we specify that we cannot
tolerate less than two replicas

00:09:51.670 --> 00:09:53.020
of our application.

00:09:53.020 --> 00:09:55.720
This is just another way
to say max unavailable

00:09:55.720 --> 00:09:59.350
equal 1 if you have three
replicas and you want only one

00:09:59.350 --> 00:10:02.320
to be unavailable.

00:10:02.320 --> 00:10:04.930
GKE respects PDB
up to 60 minutes

00:10:04.930 --> 00:10:07.900
and also termination grace
period up to 60 minutes.

00:10:07.900 --> 00:10:11.960
So that's two hours in total.

00:10:11.960 --> 00:10:14.510
As I mentioned, PDB
covers voluntary events

00:10:14.510 --> 00:10:16.460
like upgrades or repair.

00:10:16.460 --> 00:10:18.950
As you can imagine,
if there is a failure

00:10:18.950 --> 00:10:20.660
of the physical
machine, for example,

00:10:20.660 --> 00:10:25.020
PDB clearly won't be
able to mitigate that.

00:10:25.020 --> 00:10:27.780
The other thing
you want Kubernetes

00:10:27.780 --> 00:10:30.360
to know about your
workloads is whether you

00:10:30.360 --> 00:10:32.910
would benefit from
colocating some of your pods

00:10:32.910 --> 00:10:34.540
on the same machine.

00:10:34.540 --> 00:10:36.300
Or maybe, actually,
if you do so,

00:10:36.300 --> 00:10:39.390
if you do colocate some of
the pods on the same machine,

00:10:39.390 --> 00:10:41.300
you're run into risk.

00:10:41.300 --> 00:10:44.610
Kubernetes Scheduler
automatically spread your pods

00:10:44.610 --> 00:10:45.600
across nodes.

00:10:45.600 --> 00:10:47.300
However, sometimes--
for example,

00:10:47.300 --> 00:10:49.050
let's take a
stateful application

00:10:49.050 --> 00:10:51.090
like Redis or Zookeeper.

00:10:51.090 --> 00:10:54.990
A quorum of servers is required
to successfully commit mutation

00:10:54.990 --> 00:10:56.370
to data.

00:10:56.370 --> 00:10:57.870
By default,
Kubernetes may decide

00:10:57.870 --> 00:11:01.680
to colocate pods in stateful
set on the same pod.

00:11:01.680 --> 00:11:04.230
However, for
business continuity,

00:11:04.230 --> 00:11:07.350
we want to make sure
that colocated pods don't

00:11:07.350 --> 00:11:10.170
present the risk of single
point of failure, right?

00:11:10.170 --> 00:11:14.100
So if we have stateful set,
we can use pod anti-affinity,

00:11:14.100 --> 00:11:17.460
for example, to make sure that
Kubernetes don't schedule more

00:11:17.460 --> 00:11:19.330
replicas on the same machine.

00:11:19.330 --> 00:11:21.510
So each replica gets
its own machine.

00:11:21.510 --> 00:11:23.340
On the flip side,
some application

00:11:23.340 --> 00:11:25.860
may benefit from
actually being colocated.

00:11:25.860 --> 00:11:28.390
For example, let's say you
have a web server and a cache

00:11:28.390 --> 00:11:28.890
service.

00:11:28.890 --> 00:11:30.973
You want them to be on the
same machine for better

00:11:30.973 --> 00:11:34.160
performance and low latency.

00:11:34.160 --> 00:11:37.770
Donovan, are you guys using any
pod anti-affinity or affinity

00:11:37.770 --> 00:11:39.840
with your application?

00:11:39.840 --> 00:11:42.515
DONOVAN CARTER: Yeah, it's
exactly like you said, Kobi.

00:11:42.515 --> 00:11:44.640
We want to make sure our
sensitive workloads aren't

00:11:44.640 --> 00:11:47.970
exposed to single points
of failure, stateful sets

00:11:47.970 --> 00:11:49.060
specifically.

00:11:49.060 --> 00:11:51.240
But also, we use
preemptible nodes.

00:11:51.240 --> 00:11:53.280
And as you know,
preemptible nodes only last

00:11:53.280 --> 00:11:54.945
for a max of 24 hours.

00:11:54.945 --> 00:11:57.570
So we want to make sure that our
highly available workloads are

00:11:57.570 --> 00:12:00.060
truly highly available and
not subject to single points

00:12:00.060 --> 00:12:00.900
of failure.

00:12:00.900 --> 00:12:03.600
And free bonus content,
preemptible nodes

00:12:03.600 --> 00:12:06.240
are a great way to find out
if your workload is really

00:12:06.240 --> 00:12:08.747
susceptible to that or not.

00:12:08.747 --> 00:12:09.580
KOBI MAGNEZI: Right.

00:12:09.580 --> 00:12:12.040
And before we move
to cover day two,

00:12:12.040 --> 00:12:15.220
there is one more important
aspect of business continuity,

00:12:15.220 --> 00:12:19.630
and it's about having accurate
signals so Kubernetes know

00:12:19.630 --> 00:12:22.160
exactly how your
workload should behave,

00:12:22.160 --> 00:12:25.360
and it can accurately monitor
the health and the state

00:12:25.360 --> 00:12:26.470
of your workloads.

00:12:26.470 --> 00:12:28.815
The first one is
readiness probes.

00:12:28.815 --> 00:12:30.190
If your application,
for example,

00:12:30.190 --> 00:12:32.650
takes some time to
start, to initiate,

00:12:32.650 --> 00:12:34.300
you don't want
Kubernetes to start

00:12:34.300 --> 00:12:37.060
sending customer
traffic to that workload

00:12:37.060 --> 00:12:40.330
until it's fully ready
to serve traffic.

00:12:40.330 --> 00:12:44.110
You do so by setting
up the readiness probe.

00:12:44.110 --> 00:12:46.390
The other important
signal is liveness.

00:12:46.390 --> 00:12:49.360
Is your workflow
healthy and live,

00:12:49.360 --> 00:12:51.610
or does it require a repair?

00:12:51.610 --> 00:12:55.150
Setting this probe can provide
better signal to Kubernetes

00:12:55.150 --> 00:12:58.510
and also avoid false positive
cases in which Kubernetes will

00:12:58.510 --> 00:13:02.430
try to repair unnecessarily.

00:13:02.430 --> 00:13:04.210
So now that we set
up the cluster,

00:13:04.210 --> 00:13:07.650
let's cover several
recommendation for day two.

00:13:07.650 --> 00:13:09.120
So one of the most
important thing

00:13:09.120 --> 00:13:11.100
you need to carefully
plan for day two

00:13:11.100 --> 00:13:14.090
is your upgrade strategy--
and specifically,

00:13:14.090 --> 00:13:16.890
a plan to continuously
upgrade your cluster.

00:13:16.890 --> 00:13:20.010
That's because Kubernetes
minor version is released

00:13:20.010 --> 00:13:22.680
roughly every three
months from open source,

00:13:22.680 --> 00:13:25.230
and a patch version may
be released every month.

00:13:25.230 --> 00:13:27.930
Because Kubernetes run
on both the control plane

00:13:27.930 --> 00:13:30.510
and the nodes, there
are basically two places

00:13:30.510 --> 00:13:34.002
to manage when it comes
to managing versions.

00:13:34.002 --> 00:13:35.460
And there are many
reasons why it's

00:13:35.460 --> 00:13:37.860
crucial to keep your
cluster up to date.

00:13:37.860 --> 00:13:39.700
Among others is support, right?

00:13:39.700 --> 00:13:45.030
Supported versions get regular
updates and security patches.

00:13:45.030 --> 00:13:48.090
And we oftentime share
[INAUDIBLE] fixes from one

00:13:48.090 --> 00:13:49.920
supported version to another.

00:13:49.920 --> 00:13:52.380
Kubernetes release a new minor
version every three months,

00:13:52.380 --> 00:13:56.490
and it supports up to
two minor version back.

00:13:56.490 --> 00:13:59.130
That translates to roughly
supporting each minor version

00:13:59.130 --> 00:14:00.930
for about nine months.

00:14:00.930 --> 00:14:02.700
One important thing
to call out is

00:14:02.700 --> 00:14:08.370
that an open source announced
that with Kubernetes 1.19, 1.19

00:14:08.370 --> 00:14:11.100
will be the first release
to officially receive

00:14:11.100 --> 00:14:13.380
a year of patch support--

00:14:13.380 --> 00:14:15.600
so effectively prolonging
the support period

00:14:15.600 --> 00:14:19.860
from nine months to 12 months
for Kubernetes minor version.

00:14:19.860 --> 00:14:21.360
The other requirement
is to maintain

00:14:21.360 --> 00:14:23.950
supported version skew
between your control

00:14:23.950 --> 00:14:25.350
plane and the nodes.

00:14:25.350 --> 00:14:28.868
Nodes cannot run newer version
than the control plane,

00:14:28.868 --> 00:14:31.410
and they have to be within two
minor version from the control

00:14:31.410 --> 00:14:31.910
plane.

00:14:31.910 --> 00:14:35.790
So for example, if your
control plane runs on 1.19,

00:14:35.790 --> 00:14:40.590
your node pools can run 1.19,
1.18, or 1.17, the currently

00:14:40.590 --> 00:14:42.060
supported versions.

00:14:42.060 --> 00:14:45.780
Since GKE automatically upgrade
the control plane in the fleet,

00:14:45.780 --> 00:14:48.810
it is recommended to use
auto-upgrade or at least

00:14:48.810 --> 00:14:52.350
have a plan to manually upgrade
nodes on a regular basis.

00:14:52.350 --> 00:14:56.220
This way, you'll avoid getting
into an unsupported version

00:14:56.220 --> 00:15:00.480
skew situation with the
upgraded control plane.

00:15:00.480 --> 00:15:03.660
If you do decide to manage
your own upgrade workflow,

00:15:03.660 --> 00:15:06.870
you also want to make sure that
your upgrade strategy support

00:15:06.870 --> 00:15:09.510
any compliance that your
workload may be subject to.

00:15:09.510 --> 00:15:10.960
There are different compliance--

00:15:10.960 --> 00:15:13.860
PCI DSS, SOC2, and HIPAA--

00:15:13.860 --> 00:15:19.350
that Dexcom is following as
a medical devices company.

00:15:19.350 --> 00:15:21.540
Each one of these
compliance has actually

00:15:21.540 --> 00:15:24.960
specific section that speaks
about how fast you should

00:15:24.960 --> 00:15:27.122
consume critical patches.

00:15:27.122 --> 00:15:29.580
So you definitely want to take
a look into these compliance

00:15:29.580 --> 00:15:34.070
and make sure that your operate
strategy follow the compliance.

00:15:34.070 --> 00:15:36.240
In GKE, we offer fully
automated process

00:15:36.240 --> 00:15:38.880
to operate both control
plane and nodes.

00:15:38.880 --> 00:15:42.210
Control planes are
managed by Google,

00:15:42.210 --> 00:15:46.380
and we initiate their upgrade
as part of our GKE release.

00:15:46.380 --> 00:15:49.320
Your business application, the
workloads running on the nodes,

00:15:49.320 --> 00:15:52.530
stay up and running when we
upgrade the control plane.

00:15:52.530 --> 00:15:54.797
And during the
control plane upgrade,

00:15:54.797 --> 00:15:56.880
you are unable to change
the cluster configuration

00:15:56.880 --> 00:15:59.610
for several minutes because we
are taking the control plane

00:15:59.610 --> 00:16:01.800
down while bringing a new one.

00:16:01.800 --> 00:16:04.320
So when we upgrade
zonal control plane,

00:16:04.320 --> 00:16:05.910
we do it by creating
a new control

00:16:05.910 --> 00:16:07.380
plane with the new version.

00:16:07.380 --> 00:16:09.300
Then we move the network
and the disk over

00:16:09.300 --> 00:16:11.220
and delete the
old control plane.

00:16:11.220 --> 00:16:14.890
Regional control planes are
upgraded in a similar fashion,

00:16:14.890 --> 00:16:17.520
but you get the redundancies
of three control planes.

00:16:17.520 --> 00:16:20.130
So you always have two
control planes available

00:16:20.130 --> 00:16:23.330
when we upgrade one at a time.

00:16:23.330 --> 00:16:25.160
Node upgrade can be
automated as well,

00:16:25.160 --> 00:16:26.780
and we strongly
recommend you to use

00:16:26.780 --> 00:16:29.630
auto-upgrade to ensure
that your nodes are always

00:16:29.630 --> 00:16:32.510
upgraded to match your
control plane version.

00:16:32.510 --> 00:16:34.700
That approach, syncing
your control plane

00:16:34.700 --> 00:16:36.950
version and your
node pool version,

00:16:36.950 --> 00:16:39.950
not only keeping your node
infrastructure up to date,

00:16:39.950 --> 00:16:42.080
but it also mitigate
the risk of version skew

00:16:42.080 --> 00:16:44.740
that we discussed earlier.

00:16:44.740 --> 00:16:46.350
By now, you may
be wondering, why

00:16:46.350 --> 00:16:48.190
wouldn't I use auto-upgrade?

00:16:48.190 --> 00:16:49.860
It is indeed the recommendation.

00:16:49.860 --> 00:16:52.290
And while the
benefits are clear,

00:16:52.290 --> 00:16:54.930
you want to ensure
business continuity also

00:16:54.930 --> 00:16:56.850
during times of maintenance.

00:16:56.850 --> 00:17:01.290
Node upgrades used to follow
the workflow of delete

00:17:01.290 --> 00:17:01.970
and then create.

00:17:01.970 --> 00:17:04.619
That means that when we
do upgrade node pools,

00:17:04.619 --> 00:17:07.500
we delete one of the
nodes from the node pool

00:17:07.500 --> 00:17:10.960
and then we bring a new
one with the new version.

00:17:10.960 --> 00:17:13.920
We changed that workflow
recently with surge upgrade.

00:17:13.920 --> 00:17:17.430
With surge upgrade, we
create and then we delete.

00:17:17.430 --> 00:17:21.130
In other words, we bring a
new node with the new version.

00:17:21.130 --> 00:17:22.890
And only when that
node is ready,

00:17:22.890 --> 00:17:25.530
then we gracefully
drain the old node.

00:17:25.530 --> 00:17:28.530
You can actually control how
many nodes can be additionally

00:17:28.530 --> 00:17:31.920
created for surge, and
also how many existing

00:17:31.920 --> 00:17:33.540
nodes you are
willing to sacrifice

00:17:33.540 --> 00:17:35.760
for the upgrade process.

00:17:35.760 --> 00:17:39.180
The combination of
MaxSurge and MaxUnavailable

00:17:39.180 --> 00:17:41.640
also allow you to control
the pace of the upgrade.

00:17:41.640 --> 00:17:45.600
And it actually allows you to
accelerate node upgrade, which

00:17:45.600 --> 00:17:50.740
can be important especially
if you have large cluster.

00:17:50.740 --> 00:17:53.620
Donovan, I know that you guys
also have very large clusters

00:17:53.620 --> 00:17:57.363
and you are using surge upgrade
to benefit for more efficiency.

00:17:57.363 --> 00:17:59.280
DONOVAN CARTER: That's
absolutely right, Kobi.

00:17:59.280 --> 00:18:00.860
And it's like you said.

00:18:00.860 --> 00:18:04.500
We see a great increase in
the continuity of the services

00:18:04.500 --> 00:18:07.772
because they have a place to
go when that node gets taken

00:18:07.772 --> 00:18:10.230
away as opposed to having no
place to go when the node gets

00:18:10.230 --> 00:18:11.160
taken away.

00:18:11.160 --> 00:18:14.520
And also, it allows us to
do our upgrades faster.

00:18:14.520 --> 00:18:18.600
We can bring more nodes online
and remove nodes as we need to,

00:18:18.600 --> 00:18:19.590
which greatly reduces--

00:18:19.590 --> 00:18:22.000
I mean, you think about all
the places around the world

00:18:22.000 --> 00:18:23.542
that companies have
to be these days.

00:18:23.542 --> 00:18:25.950
Dexcom is no different.

00:18:25.950 --> 00:18:28.475
We certainly have
seen great benefits

00:18:28.475 --> 00:18:29.850
in terms of the
amount of time it

00:18:29.850 --> 00:18:33.030
takes to execute a
node pool upgrade

00:18:33.030 --> 00:18:36.000
thanks to surge upgrade.

00:18:36.000 --> 00:18:37.010
It's great.

00:18:37.010 --> 00:18:39.990
KOBI MAGNEZI: And we spoke
about the importance of upgrades

00:18:39.990 --> 00:18:43.020
to ensure business
continuity in day two

00:18:43.020 --> 00:18:45.720
and also the potential
disruption that these upgrades

00:18:45.720 --> 00:18:48.718
may bring to running cluster.

00:18:48.718 --> 00:18:51.010
Knowing that different
customers have different version

00:18:51.010 --> 00:18:55.810
management needs, we introduced
release channels in GKE

00:18:55.810 --> 00:18:57.640
with the objective
to actually simplify

00:18:57.640 --> 00:19:00.190
the process of choosing
your upgrade path,

00:19:00.190 --> 00:19:02.530
and also to allow you to
control the type of upgrades

00:19:02.530 --> 00:19:04.450
that you need for your cluster.

00:19:04.450 --> 00:19:05.450
Think about it this way.

00:19:05.450 --> 00:19:08.800
Some customers may want to use
the latest Kubernetes version

00:19:08.800 --> 00:19:11.110
because they have some
dependencies on newer

00:19:11.110 --> 00:19:13.450
API or capability,
or because they

00:19:13.450 --> 00:19:15.510
have some specific
business objectives.

00:19:15.510 --> 00:19:18.430
Other customers may prefer
stability over everything

00:19:18.430 --> 00:19:21.115
else, over functionality and
the freshness of a version.

00:19:21.115 --> 00:19:23.240
With release channels--
and there are three release

00:19:23.240 --> 00:19:25.210
channels, Rapid,
Regular, and Stable--

00:19:25.210 --> 00:19:29.570
you can choose where you want
to be in terms of upgrade path.

00:19:29.570 --> 00:19:31.930
So instead of choosing
a specific version,

00:19:31.930 --> 00:19:34.810
you can subscribe your cluster
to a release channel that

00:19:34.810 --> 00:19:38.020
meets your need, and GKE will
take care of the upgrade.

00:19:38.020 --> 00:19:42.250
The differences between channels
are soak time and maturity

00:19:42.250 --> 00:19:43.360
of the release.

00:19:43.360 --> 00:19:46.210
So we first introduce a
version to the Rapid channel.

00:19:46.210 --> 00:19:47.320
We let it soak.

00:19:47.320 --> 00:19:48.400
We promote it to Regular.

00:19:48.400 --> 00:19:49.540
We let it soak again.

00:19:49.540 --> 00:19:53.920
And then eventually, we
promote it to Stable.

00:19:53.920 --> 00:19:57.920
Donovan, are you using
release channels or the Rapid

00:19:57.920 --> 00:20:02.220
or the Stable channels in
your production environment?

00:20:02.220 --> 00:20:04.450
DONOVAN CARTER: I can't
go into specifics on that,

00:20:04.450 --> 00:20:08.070
but I can tell you that
release channels have greatly

00:20:08.070 --> 00:20:11.730
simplified how we evaluate
the upgrade process.

00:20:11.730 --> 00:20:14.580
Historically, we would have
to pick and choose and study

00:20:14.580 --> 00:20:16.170
release notes religiously.

00:20:16.170 --> 00:20:18.600
And now, we get a
recommendation from Google

00:20:18.600 --> 00:20:21.023
saying this has had this
amount of soak time,

00:20:21.023 --> 00:20:22.440
and we've looked
at it and believe

00:20:22.440 --> 00:20:25.212
it's the right version
for the workloads

00:20:25.212 --> 00:20:26.170
that you're looking at.

00:20:26.170 --> 00:20:28.217
So for us, we have some
sensitive workloads.

00:20:28.217 --> 00:20:30.300
We want to make sure that
they're using the Stable

00:20:30.300 --> 00:20:31.290
release channel.

00:20:31.290 --> 00:20:33.150
For other workloads
or other environments,

00:20:33.150 --> 00:20:35.550
maybe we use different
release channels

00:20:35.550 --> 00:20:38.010
that are maybe more aggressive
or have newer features

00:20:38.010 --> 00:20:40.930
so that we can do that earlier
in our development cycle.

00:20:40.930 --> 00:20:42.763
KOBI MAGNEZI: And it
is important to mention

00:20:42.763 --> 00:20:45.190
that you can unsubscribe a
cluster from release channel

00:20:45.190 --> 00:20:46.300
if you need to do that.

00:20:46.300 --> 00:20:50.520
And you can also migrate between
release channels in some cases.

00:20:50.520 --> 00:20:51.852
So this all sound great.

00:20:51.852 --> 00:20:53.060
You can automate the process.

00:20:53.060 --> 00:20:55.970
You can configure the
nature of the upgrade

00:20:55.970 --> 00:20:58.180
and control the
version maturity.

00:20:58.180 --> 00:21:01.730
However, Kubernetes upgrade
can introduce breaking changes,

00:21:01.730 --> 00:21:03.920
and they need to be
tested and validated

00:21:03.920 --> 00:21:06.290
in a preproduction
environment so that you

00:21:06.290 --> 00:21:08.370
can ensure business continuity.

00:21:08.370 --> 00:21:10.850
This is why we are
introducing available versions

00:21:10.850 --> 00:21:12.780
for channels.

00:21:12.780 --> 00:21:15.950
Each channel will include
one default version

00:21:15.950 --> 00:21:18.020
and available versions.

00:21:18.020 --> 00:21:20.750
When a version becomes
default, auto-upgrade

00:21:20.750 --> 00:21:24.080
triggers the rollout server to
promote the default versions

00:21:24.080 --> 00:21:27.620
and upgrade the control plane
first and then your node pools

00:21:27.620 --> 00:21:30.190
if you are using auto-upgrade.

00:21:30.190 --> 00:21:32.050
Before a version
become default, GKE

00:21:32.050 --> 00:21:34.660
will make it available
in the fleet.

00:21:34.660 --> 00:21:37.510
Patch versions will be available
one week before rollout.

00:21:37.510 --> 00:21:41.150
And minor version, which tend
to be a little bit more riskier,

00:21:41.150 --> 00:21:44.350
will be made available
four weeks before rollout.

00:21:44.350 --> 00:21:46.750
We recommend testing
available versions

00:21:46.750 --> 00:21:49.660
on preproduction environment
to mitigate business continuity

00:21:49.660 --> 00:21:51.640
risks and also to
ensure that when

00:21:51.640 --> 00:21:54.130
we do upgrade your
production environment,

00:21:54.130 --> 00:21:56.920
auto-upgrade can be
completed successfully.

00:21:56.920 --> 00:21:58.570
You can use the
command line that I

00:21:58.570 --> 00:22:00.790
have in the bottom
of this slide to view

00:22:00.790 --> 00:22:03.190
the default and available
versions for the release

00:22:03.190 --> 00:22:06.810
channel that you are
using for your cluster.

00:22:06.810 --> 00:22:10.260
And sometimes, disruption simply
cannot be tolerated, right?

00:22:10.260 --> 00:22:13.680
I mean, there are some
peak business seasons

00:22:13.680 --> 00:22:17.250
or major company
events like trade fair.

00:22:17.250 --> 00:22:22.530
We recommend to set up exclusion
window for these events.

00:22:22.530 --> 00:22:24.150
With exclusion window,
you can specify

00:22:24.150 --> 00:22:27.000
a range of dates in which you
want us to completely refrain

00:22:27.000 --> 00:22:28.470
from any maintenance.

00:22:28.470 --> 00:22:30.360
You can also set up
maintenance windows

00:22:30.360 --> 00:22:33.810
to say when you do prefer
maintenance to happen.

00:22:33.810 --> 00:22:35.700
That can help you
not only improve

00:22:35.700 --> 00:22:38.800
predictability of the upgrade,
but it can be very handy.

00:22:38.800 --> 00:22:41.080
Especially now when
people work from home,

00:22:41.080 --> 00:22:44.040
you might want to ensure team
availability in alignment

00:22:44.040 --> 00:22:46.170
with the working schedule
when maintenance actually

00:22:46.170 --> 00:22:46.790
takes place.

00:22:49.460 --> 00:22:55.250
Dexcom also have, I assume,
major holidays and planned

00:22:55.250 --> 00:22:57.260
peak business hours.

00:22:57.260 --> 00:22:59.760
How do you use maintenance
and exclusion windows?

00:22:59.760 --> 00:23:01.260
DONOVAN CARTER:
Yeah, it's funny you

00:23:01.260 --> 00:23:04.370
mention working from home and
the nature of the situation

00:23:04.370 --> 00:23:07.730
that we all find ourselves
in because in the olden days,

00:23:07.730 --> 00:23:10.755
we'd want to see people in
the office to manage things.

00:23:10.755 --> 00:23:13.380
And now, with working from home,
that looks a little different,

00:23:13.380 --> 00:23:15.547
but we still want to have
people awake and available

00:23:15.547 --> 00:23:20.050
and in their office, wherever
that may be, just in case.

00:23:20.050 --> 00:23:23.270
Because you want to
just be online and ready

00:23:23.270 --> 00:23:24.020
and not waking up.

00:23:24.020 --> 00:23:26.562
No one likes to wake up in the
middle of the night for things

00:23:26.562 --> 00:23:27.390
like this.

00:23:27.390 --> 00:23:30.800
And similarly, when it comes
the holidays or certain events,

00:23:30.800 --> 00:23:33.170
we find that are there
times that we would just not

00:23:33.170 --> 00:23:35.000
want to risk an
upgrade, just to make

00:23:35.000 --> 00:23:37.670
sure we provide the best quality
of service to our customers?

00:23:37.670 --> 00:23:38.240
Absolutely.

00:23:38.240 --> 00:23:41.580
Having that flexibility
has been critical for us

00:23:41.580 --> 00:23:44.322
and will continue to be critical
for us as we continue to grow.

00:23:44.322 --> 00:23:45.530
KOBI MAGNEZI: That's awesome.

00:23:45.530 --> 00:23:47.330
And I think another
recommendation

00:23:47.330 --> 00:23:49.520
that we already
kind of implicitly

00:23:49.520 --> 00:23:51.920
mentioned several
times is having

00:23:51.920 --> 00:23:53.300
multiple environments set up.

00:23:53.300 --> 00:23:55.910
I can't stress enough how
important and essential

00:23:55.910 --> 00:23:59.060
it is to have a preproduction
environment in place

00:23:59.060 --> 00:24:02.870
so you can reliably deliver
upgrades to your infrastructure

00:24:02.870 --> 00:24:06.990
and to your software safely
and in timely manner.

00:24:06.990 --> 00:24:08.900
You can think about
it in a way that you

00:24:08.900 --> 00:24:11.000
have testing
environments to test out

00:24:11.000 --> 00:24:14.660
new releases before rolling
them out to production.

00:24:14.660 --> 00:24:17.800
In this case, we recommend
subscribing both your testing

00:24:17.800 --> 00:24:20.340
cluster and your production
cluster to the same channel

00:24:20.340 --> 00:24:25.730
so you can use the same version
between testing and production.

00:24:25.730 --> 00:24:27.380
In some cases, you
may want to consider

00:24:27.380 --> 00:24:29.630
a canary or even
second development

00:24:29.630 --> 00:24:33.080
environment to test out newer
releases using the Rapid

00:24:33.080 --> 00:24:34.040
channel.

00:24:34.040 --> 00:24:36.320
That will give
you an opportunity

00:24:36.320 --> 00:24:39.170
to stay ahead of the curve
by testing out new Kubernetes

00:24:39.170 --> 00:24:42.560
features or API and
basically improve your time

00:24:42.560 --> 00:24:44.420
to market when
those versions get

00:24:44.420 --> 00:24:49.580
promoted from Rapid to Regular,
and then finally to Stable.

00:24:49.580 --> 00:24:51.740
With multiple
environments, we also

00:24:51.740 --> 00:24:54.440
need an efficient way to
orchestrate and upgrade

00:24:54.440 --> 00:24:57.440
workflow across
multiple environments.

00:24:57.440 --> 00:24:59.520
But in order to be
proactive about it,

00:24:59.520 --> 00:25:02.450
you need to be able to predict
when an upgrade is scheduled

00:25:02.450 --> 00:25:04.220
for your cluster.

00:25:04.220 --> 00:25:05.630
There's a great way to do so.

00:25:05.630 --> 00:25:09.830
We have GKE release notes
that also offer RSS feeds.

00:25:09.830 --> 00:25:11.900
You can use that.

00:25:11.900 --> 00:25:14.010
Also, if you are using
release channels,

00:25:14.010 --> 00:25:16.130
each channel offers
a separate release

00:25:16.130 --> 00:25:18.380
note so you can only subscribe
to the updates that's

00:25:18.380 --> 00:25:20.550
relevant to your cluster.

00:25:20.550 --> 00:25:22.340
You can use GKE API--

00:25:22.340 --> 00:25:24.020
specifically, Git
Server Config--

00:25:24.020 --> 00:25:27.480
to retrieve the list
of available version.

00:25:27.480 --> 00:25:29.930
These all options
are great, but they

00:25:29.930 --> 00:25:33.150
do require you to pull the
information on a regular basis.

00:25:33.150 --> 00:25:35.400
So you to be proactive about it.

00:25:35.400 --> 00:25:37.640
So to simplify this
process even more,

00:25:37.640 --> 00:25:40.190
we are introducing GKE
upgrade notification,

00:25:40.190 --> 00:25:43.850
which will be beta launched
later this quarter.

00:25:43.850 --> 00:25:46.460
Upgrade notification is
based on Cloud pub/sub.

00:25:46.460 --> 00:25:49.550
It allows customer to subscribe
to upgrade events and get

00:25:49.550 --> 00:25:52.850
automatically informed about
new available versions,

00:25:52.850 --> 00:25:54.950
and also when the
upgrade actually

00:25:54.950 --> 00:25:57.830
takes place on their cluster.

00:25:57.830 --> 00:26:00.530
In this case that I
have here in this slide,

00:26:00.530 --> 00:26:04.070
we have a devops steam
using a Slack channel.

00:26:04.070 --> 00:26:07.700
And [INAUDIBLE] is being
pushed to their Slack channel

00:26:07.700 --> 00:26:10.220
through the pub/sub
subscription.

00:26:10.220 --> 00:26:13.190
And the team, this way, know
about and notified ahead

00:26:13.190 --> 00:26:15.860
of time about new versions.

00:26:15.860 --> 00:26:17.660
This way, the team
can optionally

00:26:17.660 --> 00:26:20.105
orchestrate a specific
workflow-- for example,

00:26:20.105 --> 00:26:21.730
upgrade their testing
environment first

00:26:21.730 --> 00:26:23.270
to validate their
release, make sure

00:26:23.270 --> 00:26:27.260
that their release is
ready to be safely roll out

00:26:27.260 --> 00:26:30.060
into production environment.

00:26:30.060 --> 00:26:31.530
So let's put
everything together.

00:26:31.530 --> 00:26:33.380
We spoke about
different concepts.

00:26:33.380 --> 00:26:35.840
A recommended approach to
automate and streamline

00:26:35.840 --> 00:26:39.200
upgrades would involve
preproduction environment,

00:26:39.200 --> 00:26:42.590
using the available version
that we mentioned before,

00:26:42.590 --> 00:26:46.190
and upgrade notification that's
set up on your cluster that

00:26:46.190 --> 00:26:50.420
will inform your team when
a new available version is

00:26:50.420 --> 00:26:52.550
ready for them to
test and certify,

00:26:52.550 --> 00:26:55.040
and eventually, a
production environment

00:26:55.040 --> 00:26:58.160
subscribed to a release channel
using the default version

00:26:58.160 --> 00:27:00.140
in that channel.

00:27:00.140 --> 00:27:02.080
DONOVAN CARTER: So
we've covered a lot.

00:27:02.080 --> 00:27:03.788
And if you can believe
it, we've actually

00:27:03.788 --> 00:27:05.210
got more we could cover.

00:27:05.210 --> 00:27:07.670
But we hope that you've gotten
a sense of some of the best

00:27:07.670 --> 00:27:10.400
practices and recommendations,
both from real-world experience

00:27:10.400 --> 00:27:13.160
as well as from Google and
Google's experience managing

00:27:13.160 --> 00:27:16.100
GKE, to know enough to be
really, really dangerous when

00:27:16.100 --> 00:27:18.590
it comes to running a
highly available, really

00:27:18.590 --> 00:27:23.690
tough with strict SLOs/SLAs
product in Google Kubernetes

00:27:23.690 --> 00:27:24.840
Engine.

00:27:24.840 --> 00:27:27.770
There are a lot of things
on this slide that we could

00:27:27.770 --> 00:27:30.920
talk at great length about,
but we won't, per the interest

00:27:30.920 --> 00:27:31.720
of time.

00:27:31.720 --> 00:27:33.170
But we also just
want to thank you

00:27:33.170 --> 00:27:35.627
real quick for spending
your time with us

00:27:35.627 --> 00:27:37.460
and getting to hear a
little bit about this.

00:27:37.460 --> 00:27:38.990
And I really
